# stage0: the first stage of a SemanticScript compiler written in SemanticScript.
#
#     cd docs/refined-examples/stage0
#     refined build ../../../semanticscript/runtime --kind runtime --output build/runtime.o
#     refined run . -- samples/hello build/hello gcc build/runtime.o \
#         -nostdlib -nostartfiles -Wl,-e,refined_entry -lkernel32 -lgcc     (Windows)
#         -nostdlib -nostartfiles -static -fno-stack-protector -lgcc       (Linux)
#
# It reads the project directory named by the first argument: its build.sem
# names the modules (`includesModule`, in order) and the entry
# (`<module>.main`). It reads each module's file, `<module>.sem` in the
# project directory, and puts their texts one after another, after a module of
# its own, `string`, which declares the standard library's string.Position
# record. It tokenizes that program text (lexer), groups the tokens into rows
# of words (syntax), groups the rows into entities (model), checks module by
# module that the program is in the stage0 subset (subset), writes it as C
# (emitc) to the second argument with `.c` added, and runs the C compiler named
# by the third argument (gcc when there is none) to build the second argument
# as an executable. Every argument after the compiler's name is passed to it
# after the C file: the runtime of refined executables (the runtime in
# SemanticScript as an object), which the C file is linked with, and the
# flags that link it with no C library (D-142).
#
# A diagnostic names the file the word is in and the line within that file:
# each file's text starts at a known offset and line of the program text.
#
# Authority (build.sem, and the capabilities below): read the command line;
# read files under the working directory; write files under `build/`; run a C
# compiler named exactly `cc`, `gcc`, or `clang`. Paths come from the command
# line, so their scopes are checked when the files are opened (D-38); a path
# outside them stops the program before the file is touched.
#
# Exit codes: 0 built; 1 the program is refused, with a diagnostic
# `path:line:column: message (word)` on standard error; 2 usage; 3 a file could
# not be read or written, or a source is not UTF-8; 4 the C compiler could not
# be run or failed; 5 standard output failed.
#
# This module is generated from tools/stage0.dsl by tools/build.py.